diff --git a/docs/scheduled-exports.md b/docs/scheduled-exports.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..59cc5af
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/scheduled-exports.md
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# Scheduled exports
+
+A scheduled export copies one of your datasets into a bucket you own, on a
+cadence you choose. We only ever write to the bucket named in the schedule.
+
+## Creating a schedule
+
+```
+POST /v1/export-schedules
+{
+  "name": "nightly events",
+  "dataset": "events", "format": "csv",
+  "cadence": "daily", "hour_of_day": 3, "timezone": "Europe/Berlin",
+  "destination": {
+    "bucket": "acme-exports", "prefix": "analytics/", "region": "eu-west-1",
+    "access_key": "...", "secret_key": "..."
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`dataset` is one of `events`, `sessions` or `conversions`; `format` is `csv` or
+`jsonl`; `cadence` is `hourly`, `daily` or `weekly`. The destination
+credentials are stored encrypted and are never handed back by the API, so keep
+your own copy — rotating them means recreating the schedule.
+
+`hour_of_day` is interpreted in `timezone`, which must be an IANA name such as
+`America/New_York` and defaults to UTC. Weekly schedules run on Monday at that
+hour; hourly schedules ignore `hour_of_day` and run on the hour.
+
+## What lands in your bucket
+
+Each run writes one object under `prefix`, named for the dataset and the moment
+the run started:
+
+```
+analytics/events-2024-05-02.csv
+```
+
+CSV files carry a header row of column names; JSONL files carry one object per
+line with those same keys and the same values. Timestamps are RFC 3339 in UTC.
+
+A run covers exactly the interval since the run before it — the last hour, day
+or week of `occurred_at` — so consecutive objects tile the timeline. Concatenate
+everything a schedule has produced and you have the dataset with no gaps and no
+duplicated rows, and no run overwrites an object an earlier run wrote.
+
+## Failures
+
+The worker polls every 30 seconds and takes whatever is due; a schedule is
+claimed by one worker, so a due run happens once even with several workers
+live. A run that fails — bad credentials, an unreachable bucket, a query that
+times out — leaves `last_status` as `failed` and `last_error` set on the
+schedule, and the schedule moves on to its next slot. We do not retry inside
+the window, so a hard failure means one missing file.
+
+`GET /v1/export-schedules` returns your schedules with those fields, and is the
+first place to look when a file you expected has not appeared. `DELETE
+/v1/export-schedules/{id}` stops future runs; objects already in your bucket
+are left alone.
